{
 "_note": "THE on:scroll / on:scrollEnd / on:reachEnd PLANE. Three independent laws in one file because one dispatch decides all three. (1) MOTION is derived from a SAMPLE PAIR, never from an accumulated delta: dx/dy, a per-axis velocity in points per second, the DOMINANT-axis direction word, and a scalar `velocity` signed along the axis `direction` names, so the two are one coherent statement. Direction is STICKY below the half-point tolerance — a one-pixel jitter that flips `direction` every frame is exactly the shrinking-header flicker, so a sub-tolerance sample keeps the previous word instead of inventing `none`. A non-monotonic or backwards clock yields velocity 0 rather than an infinity. (2) COALESCING is the performance contract: `on:scroll` NEVER dispatches when no handler is bound (zero, not cheap), and otherwise at most once per display-link tick (16 ms default). The corpus asserts the DISPATCH COUNT over a fixed sample train, so a regression is caught rather than felt. (3) reachEnd is EDGE-TRIGGERED through a latch: it fires on the sample that crosses the threshold, not on every frame spent at the bottom, and the latch releases when the user scrolls back out. Threshold is in points and the sub-pixel tolerance applies, so a rail reached to within a quarter point counts. ORDERING within one dispatch is scroll -> reachEnd, so a handler that appends rows sees the position that triggered it; scrollEnd is a separate settle signal (SETTLE_MS = 120 on renderers with no platform deceleration callback).",
